WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:08.080
Logical operators involve using the keywords
and, or, and not to compare two or more conditions

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.550
and then return a true or false.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.790
As with many types of operators, the order
of operations plays a factor in a result,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.529
as we will see in an upcoming example.

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:22.770
For this code example, 143-logical, we have
a tuple that shows some gases in the periodic

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.320
table of elements on line 1.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.369
On lines 2 and 3, we have variables storing
the number of gases and the number of liquids

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.180
in the table.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.770
On lines five and seven, we have print statements
with logical keywords in use.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.579
Let’s analyze the statement on line 5.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:50.320
Look for the and keyword and then test the
logic of the conditions on either side of

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.670
the keyword.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.280
Is sodium in gases?

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.890
No.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:02.670
Right away, this statement is false because
for an and statement to be true, both parts

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.070
of it need to be true.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:11.040
I will run this code, and indeed this statement
is false.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:18.100
Before we move on to the next print statement,
let’s add the word not in front of the in

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.490
keyword.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:24.270
Sodium is not a gas, so this part of the statement
is true.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.049
Let’s analyze the other part of the statement.

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:36.260
There are fewer liquids than gases, so this
expression is also true, and when I rerun

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.329
the code, the statement is indeed true.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:45.530
Let’s now analyze the print statement on
line 7.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.049
Is hydrogen a gas?

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:50.320
Yes.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:59.520
Helium is also a gas, but this statement would
be true anyway because, in an or situation,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.039
only one side of the expression needs to be
true for the or statement to be true.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:14.180
Next, we see an and statement, and the number
of liquids is not 4, so this should be false.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.990
Yet, when I run this code, this statement
is true.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.640
Why is that?

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.780
To know why this statement returns true, we
need to understand the order of operations

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.040
as it pertains to logical statements.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.599
Not is always evaluated first.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.040
Then, and is evaluated before not.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:42.379
Thus, we evaluated the statement left to right
in this case, but this part, helium in gases

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:47.670
and number of liquids equaling 4, was evaluated
first.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.519
That part is false.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:56.050
But then, when you evaluate this with hydrogen
being a gas, the statement as a whole becomes

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.670
true because hydrogen is a gas.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:04.130
And, what did we say about an or statement?

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:10.080
Only one side of the statement needs to be
true for the whole statement to be true.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.640
As we will see in the upcoming topic on arithmetic,
adding parentheses to any part of a statement

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.950
can dramatically affect the results of said
statement.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:23.640
I will wrap parentheses around the two gases.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.140
What do you think the result will be now?

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:34.580
This part is true, but the whole expression
compared to this and statement should return

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.640
false because this part is false.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:45.930
And, in rerunning the code, the statement
as a whole indeed is false.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.440
Besides learning about logical operators,
we have seen the importance of order of operations

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.700
and how parentheses can shape the logic of
a statement.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.880
Not knowing these factors leads to logical
errors and lots of frustration as to why code

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.480
seemingly does not work, even though technically,
it does work.

